Root cause fixed: **USART2 interrupt was never enabled in NVIC**, so peripheral RXNE set in `USART_SR`, but the IRQ was blocked before reaching the CPU handler.

### What was wrong
In `usart_setup()` the code had a comment “Enable the USART2 interrupt” but no actual NVIC call.  
That creates exactly the observed behavior:

- `RXNE` bit sets when a byte arrives ✅
- `usart2_isr()` never executes ❌
- no echo transmitted ❌

### Fix applied
Updated file:
`firmware/libopencm3-examples/examples/stm32/f4/stm32f4-discovery/usart_irq/usart_irq.c`

Added:
```c
/* Route USART2 interrupt line from peripheral to Cortex-M NVIC. */
nvic_enable_irq(NVIC_USART2_IRQ);
```
directly in `usart_setup()` before USART operation.

### Result
With `RXNEIE` already enabled and handler present (`usart2_isr`), incoming bytes now generate the interrupt path correctly, allowing LED toggle and echo logic to run.

### Note on local build
A full local rebuild is currently blocked in this environment by missing libopencm3 path configuration (`OPENCM3_DIR` not set), but the code-level interrupt routing defect is corrected.
